// PARAM: --analysis interval
// Adapted from Goblint tests/regression/56-witness/26-mine-tutorial-ex4.6.c
// (Mine's tutorial, example 4.6): a counter runs from 40 down to 0 and
// exits on x != 0. Goblint proves the in-loop bounds only by unassuming
// a YAML witness (26-mine-tutorial-ex4.6.yml); the post-loop x == 0 needs
// no witness.
//
// Voblint proves x == 0 and neither in-loop bound. Mechanism: the head
// widens x from [40,40] against [39,40] and drops the lower bound; the
// guard !(x == 0) is a disequality that gives intervals no floor to narrow
// back to. The widened lower bound is not an int32, so the decrement
// x := x - 1 reaches the conversion's give-up branch and answers the whole
// int32 range, taking the upper bound 40 with it -- which is why !(40 < x)
// is undecided here too, and not only !(x < 0).
//
// Saturating the widening at int32's own minimum, as Goblint's interval
// widening does, does not recover the upper bound: [-2147483648, 40] - 1
// underflows just the same and the wrapped result has no single-interval
// representation but the whole range. The example is Mine's, stated over
// the mathematical integers; under sem.int.signed_overflow=assume_none the
// decrement would keep [-2147483649, 39] and 40 would survive. VIMP freezes
// wraparound instead.
//
// x == 0 holds because the exit edge asserts equality, which no widening
// touches.
// Solver-invariant across join, per-origin, warrow, warrow-per-origin.
// The same program under --analysis int (16-composite-domain/precision/
// 04-mine_ex46_sign_zero_threshold.vimp) proves all three.
void main() {
  int32 x;
  x := 40;
  while (!(x == 0)) {
    __voblint_check(!(40 < x)); // UNKNOWN
    x := x - 1;
    __voblint_check(!(x < 0)) // UNKNOWN
  };
  __voblint_check(x == 0) // PROVED
}
